        <div class="card-institutional bg-cream-100 border-l-4 border-signal-600 text-sm text-graphite">
          <strong class="text-navy-900">Editorial framework, not a quote.</strong> Retention percentages, spread figures and offer bands below describe observed patterns on TheResaleValue valuations and are illustrative. Actual bids depend on the specific car, its condition, and the city and week of sale.
        </div>

        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">At five years, roughly 20 percentage points separate the strongest-holding Maruti from the weakest-holding one. Maruti's brand average against Hyundai's brand average? Closer to 5. That bit of arithmetic reverses the advice most buyers act on. Picking the right Maruti model matters roughly four times more to the resale outcome than picking Maruti over a rival badge.</p>

        <p>This page carries a 10-model retention table, segment-level retention curves from Year 1 to Year 7, variant-level spread inside the same model, and the city effects that swing the same car's bid by tens of thousands of rupees between Delhi and Kochi. A <a href="/valuation">free used car valuation</a> will give a number for one specific car. This page explains why that number lands where it does.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Maruti Suzuki's resale premium is a liquidity premium: what buyers are actually paying for</h2>

        <p>Maruti resale value in India is high mainly because Maruti cars sell fast, not because they last longer than everything else. The premium is a liquidity premium: buyers pay extra for a car they know they can exit quickly, at a predictable price, to a large pool of second-hand buyers spread across metros, tier-2 and tier-3 towns.</p>

        <p>Liquidity premium, in plain language, means the extra amount a buyer will pay because the car can be resold again easily. A share that trades every second is worth more than an identical share nobody buys. Used cars work the same way.</p>

        <p>Three mechanisms produce it. The first is buyer-pool depth. A used Swift listed in Nashik, Hubballi or Muzaffarpur draws enquiries within days, because there is a service point within reach and every mechanic on the highway has opened up a K-series engine at some point. A European-badged hatchback listed in the same town often draws none, and the seller ends up shipping it to Pune or Bengaluru to find a buyer at all.</p>

        <p>Repair-cost certainty is the second. A used-car buyer inspecting a WagonR can predict what a clutch job costs, what a set of front struts costs, what a windshield costs. That predictability gets priced in. Uncertainty about a future repair bill is always discounted at purchase.</p>

        <p>Third is exit certainty. Organised buyers, whether that is Maruti True Value, the platform buyers, or the local multi-brand dealer on the arterial road, will always bid on a clean Maruti. That creates a floor. Even a poorly presented car has a buyer at some price, which means the seller is never negotiating from zero.</p>

        <p>Most people read Maruti's resale premium as a durability signal. That is only half the story. Several non-Maruti models are mechanically comparable or better and still retain less, purely because their resale queue is shorter. Which sets up the point that follows: the Maruti premium collapses on Maruti models whose own buyer pool is thin.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">The intra-brand spread: why the gap between the best and worst Maruti is wider than the Maruti-vs-Hyundai gap</h2>

        <p>Choosing the right Maruti model matters more for resale than choosing Maruti over another brand. The spread between the best-holding and weakest-holding Maruti at five years is around 20 percentage points, while the Maruti-versus-Hyundai brand-average spread sits near 5. Model choice, not badge choice, drives the outcome.</p>

        <p>Four variables create that spread. None of them is build quality.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">Body style demand.</strong> Compact SUV and MPV bodies have a wider second-hand audience today than mid-size sedans do. A Brezza and a Ciaz of identical age and condition are competing for very different queues.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">Fuel option availability.</strong> A model offered with factory CNG has a demand layer the petrol-only version does not: commercial users, high-running private owners, and buyers in Gujarat, Delhi-NCR and coastal Maharashtra where CNG infrastructure is dense.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">New-car discount depth.</strong> When a model carries heavy scheme support at the dealership, the price of the equivalent one-year-old car has to sit meaningfully below the discounted new price. Discounting on the new car quietly drags the used car down with it.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">Finance-driven new-car substitution.</strong> Under-discussed, this one. When a brand-new entry hatchback is available at a very low monthly EMI with a small down payment, a buyer who was considering a four-year-old version of that same car often buys new instead. The used queue for entry models thins out because the alternative is a new car with a warranty at a similar monthly outgo. That mechanism is why entry hatchbacks sit at the bottom of the retention table despite being the brand's most reliable products.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Which Maruti has the best resale value: the five models that clear the mid-50s at five years</h2>

        <p>The Maruti Swift holds value best over five years, followed closely by the Baleno, Brezza, Ertiga and the factory-CNG WagonR. All five clear the mid-50s in percentage retention at five years and average kilometres. Each one holds because of a specific demand mechanism, not because of a spec-sheet advantage.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Swift: the resale benchmark the whole segment is priced against</h3>

        <p>The Swift is the reference price for the used premium-hatchback band. Dealers quote other hatchbacks relative to it: "₹40,000 under a same-year Swift" is an actual sentence used at procurement desks. That reference status feeds itself, because a car everyone prices against is a car everyone can price quickly.</p>

        <p>Retention runs roughly 68 to 72% at three years and 55 to 60% at five years at average kilometres.</p>

        <p>Fuel history matters more than most sellers realise. The Swift has been petrol-only since the 2020 BS6 transition, so any diesel Swift in the market is a pre-2020 DDiS car. In Delhi-NCR those cars ran into the vehicle-age restriction well before their mechanical life ended, and their values reset accordingly. A 2013 or 2015 DDiS Swift in Delhi trades on its value in Jaipur or Lucknow, not in Delhi.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Baleno: the Nexa-channel premium hatchback and its narrower buyer pool</h3>

        <p>The Baleno retains strongly in percentage terms, roughly 66 to 70% at three years and 54 to 58% at five. But the absolute prices are higher, so the rupee loss is larger than the Swift's even when the percentage looks similar. A seller comparing the two should be clear about which number matters to them.</p>

        <p>Its buyer pool is also narrower at the top end. A fully loaded Baleno's used price starts overlapping with entry compact SUVs, and at that overlap point a chunk of buyers walks across the segment line. That ceiling is what the variant section below deals with in detail.</p>

        <p>Buyers in Hyderabad often find Baleno inventory moves faster than in tier-3 Telangana towns, where the Nexa service question comes up during the negotiation itself.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Brezza: the compact SUV that keeps a floor under itself</h3>

        <p>The Brezza holds because the body style holds. Compact SUV demand in the used market has been the strongest structural trend of the last five years, and the Brezza sits in the middle of the band where budgets concentrate.</p>

        <p>Retention is roughly 67 to 71% at three years and 56 to 60% at five.</p>

        <p>The diesel story splits by geography, and this is where the same car is not the same asset. The 1.3 DDiS Brezza built before the petrol-only switch is effectively locked out of Delhi-NCR once it crosses the age line, so NCR bids on those cars get quoted assuming an interstate sale. In Kerala and interior Maharashtra, the same diesel Brezza retains meaningfully better, because there is no age restriction and running-cost economics still favour diesel over long daily distances. Kochi is different again: a large share of buyers there run 60 to 80 km daily commutes on state highways, and diesel demand has not softened the way it has up north.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ertiga: the MPV with a second, non-retail demand source</h3>

        <p>Everything above competes for private buyers only. The Ertiga does not. It has a second bidder class in tourist-permit operators, corporate transport fleets, airport and hotel contract runs, and that layer puts a hard floor under 5 to 7-year values that no hatchback enjoys.</p>

        <p>Retention sits around 70 to 73% at three years and 58 to 62% at five, and the seven-year figure holds up better than anything else in the range.</p>

        <p>The practical effect shows up in cities with heavy contract-transport activity. In Bengaluru, where IT-park employee transport runs on MPVs, a high-kilometre Ertiga that a private buyer would reject outright still gets a serious fleet bid.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">WagonR CNG: the factory-fitted CNG anomaly</h3>

        <p>The WagonR is usually filed as a resale laggard. That is half the picture. The petrol WagonR and the factory-fitted S-CNG WagonR do not behave like the same car in the used market.</p>

        <p>Factory CNG carries a resale premium. An aftermarket kit generally does not, and can subtract value. Two reasons for that. The factory car's CNG system is endorsed on the Registration Certificate from day one and is covered under the manufacturer warranty history, so there is no paperwork risk for the next buyer. An aftermarket installation has to be endorsed on the RC separately, and when that endorsement is missing, the buyer inherits a compliance problem and a possible insurance complication.</p>

        <p>Delhi-NCR and Gujarat carry the strongest CNG bids, and the premium narrows sharply in cities with thin CNG station coverage. The fuel-type layer is covered in detail in <a href="/blogs/petrol-diesel-cng-resale-value-india">petrol, diesel and CNG resale differences in 2026</a>.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Alto, S-Presso and Ciaz: the three weakest Maruti resale performers and the structural reason behind each</h2>

        <p>A low retention percentage does not indicate a mechanical problem. The Alto K10, S-Presso and Ciaz retain least among mainstream Maruti models, and in each case the cause is structural, low new-car price, a young secondary market, or a shrinking body style, not build quality.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Alto: low new-car price is the cause, not low quality</h3>

        <p>The percentage-versus-rupees trap catches almost every Alto seller. When the new price is small, an ordinary rupee drop reads as a large percentage drop. A ₹60,000 fall on a ₹5 lakh car is 12%; the identical ₹60,000 fall on a ₹12 lakh car is 5%. The Alto is not depreciating harder in absolute money. It is depreciating from a smaller base.</p>

        <p>Finance substitution compounds it. A new Alto K10 at a low EMI competes directly with a four-year-old Alto K10, and enough buyers choose new that the used queue stays short.</p>

        <p>Then there is the age horizon. The oldest Alto examples on the road are approaching the 15-year mark in NCR, and once a car sits within two or three years of a de-registration limit, bids in that region drop steeply regardless of condition.</p>

        <p>Five-year retention typically lands in the low 40s.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">S-Presso: a thin secondary market because the model is still young</h3>

        <p>The S-Presso's production history is short, which means fewer comparable transactions, which means wider price uncertainty, which means conservative dealer bids. That chain is the whole explanation.</p>

        <p>Dealers price against comparables. When only a handful of same-year, same-variant S-Presso sales exist in a given market, the desk protects itself by bidding low and holding a bigger margin buffer. This is a market-maturity effect, and it narrows as volume accumulates. The same pattern showed up on the Ignis and on early Brezza stock before their comparable sets filled out.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ciaz: the mid-size sedan segment contraction</h3>

        <p>The Ciaz's retention curve tracks its body style, not its badge. Used mid-size sedan demand has been absorbed almost entirely by compact SUVs, because at any given used budget a buyer can now choose between a sedan and an SUV of similar age, and a large majority chooses the SUV for ground clearance and perceived resale.</p>

        <p>That is a segment-wide movement affecting every mid-size sedan in the used market, regardless of manufacturer. Worth noting that Ciaz owners often report strong ownership satisfaction; retention and satisfaction are measuring different things entirely.</p>

        <p>Where the Ciaz does hold better is in markets with heavy self-drive and outstation taxi use. In Chennai, contract sedans still move, and a well-kept Ciaz finds a bidder faster than the national average would suggest.</p>

