        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">How to read this table.</strong> Retention = actual resale transaction price divided by original ex-showroom price, measured at the average kilometre reading for that age band. Kilometres and age do not move value at the same rate; each has its own curve. The Fronx and Grand Vitara are excluded from the five-year column because neither has enough five-year transactions in the underlying dataset to publish a defensible figure yet.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Nexa vs Arena resale value: does the showroom channel change what a used Maruti sells for?</h2>

        <p>The Nexa versus Arena channel does not itself create resale value. Price band does. Nexa models sit at higher price points where the used buyer pool is smaller and the rupee depreciation is larger, even when percentage retention looks similar to an Arena model.</p>

        <p>For readers unfamiliar with the split: Maruti Suzuki retails through two networks. Arena carries the mass-market range, so Swift, WagonR, Alto K10, Dzire, Ertiga, Brezza. Nexa carries the premium-positioned range: Baleno, Fronx, Grand Vitara, Ignis, XL6, Jimny. Same manufacturer, same parts supply chain, different showroom experience and different price positioning.</p>

        <p>In the used market, the badge on the showroom does not reach the buyer. The price band does. At ₹6 lakh, the pool of buyers is enormous. At ₹11 lakh it is a fraction of that size, and every additional lakh narrows it further. A car in a narrow band sits longer, and inventory that sits longer gets bid lower at procurement.</p>

        <p>Service access is the second variable, and it is geographic. Arena workshops are far more widely distributed than Nexa workshops, so a Nexa-model owner in a district town may be driving further for scheduled service. Used buyers in those markets factor that in. The effect is negligible in Mumbai, Pune or Chennai, where both networks are dense. It becomes visible in tier-3 procurement.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">The variant trap: why a top-trim Maruti loses more rupees than a base-trim one</h2>

        <p>Used car buyers shop by budget band, not by feature list. Someone with ₹5.5 lakh looks at everything priced between ₹5.2 and ₹5.8 lakh, then picks the best car in that window. Nobody searches for "ZXi+". That is the mechanism behind the variant trap.</p>

        <p>Work through a Baleno. Suppose the gap between an LXi and a ZXi+ is around ₹2.3 lakh when new. At five years, the same-year, same-kilometre pair typically sits about ₹80,000 to ₹1,00,000 apart. That is a recovery ratio near 35 to 40% on the feature premium. The base car recovered a higher share of what its owner paid.</p>

        <p>What does recover reasonably well: an AMT or automatic gearbox, factory-fitted CNG, and, in the compact SUV band specifically, a sunroof, which used buyers actively filter for. What recovers little: paint packages, dealer-fitted accessory bundles, cosmetic body kits, extended feature packs that do not change the driving experience.</p>

        <p>The AMT premium is geographically uneven, and that is worth planning around at purchase. Bengaluru and Mumbai used-car demand for automatics is materially stronger than in most tier-2 markets, because the daily driving is stop-start and buyers there have already decided they want two pedals. Bengaluru is different again because the ORR commute has turned AMT into a search filter rather than a nice-to-have.</p>

        <p>The practical rule: mid-trim usually recovers best. It clears the safety and convenience features buyers actually filter for, without paying for the top-trim items that do not survive into the used-market price.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">What a full inspection finds on used Maruti models, and how each finding is priced into the offer</h2>

        <p>Two Swifts, same year, same 62,000 km, same colour. One gets an offer ₹70,000 higher than the other. The difference is almost never anything the seller was aware of.</p>

        <p>Every finding below feeds into the same pricing logic covered in <a href="/blogs/factors-affecting-used-car-resale-value">the 10 factors that decide resale value</a>. This section is the operational layer underneath it.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">The findings that quietly cost the most money</h3>

        <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Paint thickness readings.</strong> A gauge reading well above factory range on a single panel indicates a repaint. On a door or a fender, that is a modest deduction. On a pillar or a quarter panel, it suggests structural repair, the deduction multiplies, and some buyers will not bid at all.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Suspension bush and strut wear.</strong> High-kilometre hatchbacks driven on Mumbai's east-west link roads or Bengaluru's older arterials show worn front strut mounts and control arm bushes far earlier than the same model in, say, Chandigarh. Road surface is a resale variable that no brochure mentions.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Clutch travel on manual city cars.</strong> A Swift or a Dzire that spent its life in Bengaluru peak-hour traffic frequently shows a high biting point at 60,000 km. It is a known, priceable job, and it gets priced in.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">AMT actuator behaviour.</strong> On Celerio, WagonR and Alto AMT cars, the inspection checks shift smoothness under load and any hesitation on the 1-2 upshift. Actuator replacement costs significantly more than a clutch job, so a hesitant AMT moves the offer sharply.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Timing chain noise on older 1.3 DDiS units.</strong> Older diesel Swifts, Dzires and Brezzas get listened to at cold start specifically for chain rattle.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Cabin electricals on older top trims.</strong> Touchscreen units, reverse camera feeds and power-window modules on 6 to 8-year-old ZXi+ cars fail more often than the mechanicals do, and because these are top-trim features, the deduction lands on the car that cost more when new.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">The paperwork findings buyers never think about</h3>

        <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Missing service history for the middle years.</strong> Full history for years 1, 2 and year 6, nothing in between, reads as neglect regardless of the car's actual condition.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">RC fuel type not matching the actual setup.</strong> Most common on aftermarket CNG cars where the endorsement was never done. This is a compliance issue for the next owner, not a bargaining chip.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Hypothecation not removed after loan closure.</strong> The RC still names the financier. Until that is cleared with the NOC from the bank and the RTO endorsement, transfer is blocked.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Address mismatch between RC and current city.</strong> A Pune-registered car being sold in Hyderabad by an owner who moved years ago adds an interstate step to the transfer.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Pending challans against the registration number.</strong> These surface instantly on a VAHAN check and get netted off the offer.</li>
        </ul>

