        </div>

        <p>The flattening matters for one specific decision: sell now or hold one more year. Between Year 1 and Year 3, a hatchback shedding 10-plus points annually makes waiting expensive. Between Year 5 and Year 6, the same car might shed 5 or 6 points, at which point the cost of holding sits closer to the cost of a year's insurance and servicing, and the calculation changes.</p>

        <p>Compact SUVs behave differently, and this trips up Brezza owners. The curve does not flatten as sharply, so "one more year" carries a steadier cost right through Year 7. The fuel-type layer sits on top of all of this. CNG and diesel variants follow their own curves, covered separately.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Maruti vs Hyundai, Honda and Tata: where the brand premium actually appears</h2>

        <p>Maruti resale value is strongest against comparable models in the entry and compact segments, and the gap narrows considerably in the compact SUV band, where several competing models have built strong independent used-market demand. The difference is a demand and network effect, not a judgement on product quality.</p>

