        </div>

        <p>Why the SUV band is close is straightforward enough. Compact SUVs from Tata and Hyundai have accumulated enough used-market volume, service coverage and buyer familiarity that their resale queues are now deep too. Liquidity is not exclusive to one manufacturer. It gets earned through volume and network density, and several models have earned it.</p>

        <p>Where the Maruti advantage stays widest is in smaller towns. In a district headquarters town in Bihar or eastern UP, the service network available to a used-car buyer is a genuine constraint, and Maruti's presence there is dense. That geography is where the retention gap shows up most clearly, and it is why national averages understate the difference for buyers outside the metros.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">City effect: how Delhi's diesel age rule and Bengaluru's demand queue change the same Maruti's resale value</h2>

        <p>The same 2019 Brezza, same 55,000 km, same condition, gets four different bids in four cities. Regional patterns like this are unpacked further in <a href="/blogs/used-car-resale-value-by-city-india">city-wise used car resale premiums</a>.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">Delhi-NCR.</strong> Vehicle-age restrictions in the National Capital Region mean diesel cars beyond a defined age and petrol cars beyond a longer defined age cannot be operated or re-registered there. In plain language: once a car crosses the limit, its Delhi resale market disappears, and its value is set by whatever a buyer in another state will pay after re-registration costs. A diesel car three years short of the limit already trades at a discount, because buyers price in the shortened usable life.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">Bengaluru.</strong> Demand-side pressure rather than regulation. Automatic and AMT cars command a wider premium than the national average, and clean single-owner hatchbacks clear quickly because of continuous inbound migration. The queue of buyers is long; the queue of good cars is not.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">Kochi and coastal markets.</strong> Diesel demand holds better, but corrosion findings are more common and get checked more aggressively at inspection: underbody, rear beam, door bottoms. A coastal car with visible surface rust takes a deduction a Delhi car of the same age would not.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">Pune.</strong> Works differently again. Proximity to Mumbai means cars move between the two markets constantly, so pricing tracks Mumbai closely, but registration transfer between MH-12 and MH-01/02 series is intra-state and therefore simpler than a genuine interstate move.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">The interstate route.</strong> When a car cannot be re-registered in its home state, it gets sold into another one. That requires an NOC, a no-objection certificate from the originating RTO confirming there are no dues or cases against the vehicle, followed by re-registration at the destination RTO, and in most cases a road tax payment in the new state with a refund claim filed against the old one. The refund process is separate, slower, and frequently abandoned by sellers. Buyers price all of this in, which is why an out-of-state car sells below an equivalent local-registration car.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Five decisions made at purchase that set a Maruti's resale value years later</h2>

        <ol class="list-decimal ml-6 space-y-3">
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Variant.</strong> Within any budget, the mid-trim recovers best. It carries the features used buyers filter for, without the top-trim premium that does not survive depreciation.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Fuel and transmission, matched to the city.</strong> Factory CNG is worth ordering if the car will live in Delhi-NCR, Gujarat or coastal Maharashtra where station density is high and CNG demand is deep. An AMT is worth the premium in Bengaluru or Mumbai. Neither holds the same value in a market where the demand is not there.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Colour.</strong> White and silver clear fastest in the Maruti used market and take the smallest condition-adjusted discount. Unusual shades lengthen days-to-sell, and days-to-sell shows up in the acquisition price.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Service discipline from year one.</strong> A complete authorised-workshop record removes the single largest deduction a buyer can impose for uncertainty. Missing history in the middle years costs more than the servicing itself would have.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy-900">Exit timing.</strong> On hatchbacks, selling before the year-5 to year-6 crossover captures the flattening curve. On compact SUVs, the curve does not flatten as sharply, so the calculation is different. On diesels registered in NCR, the answer is unambiguous: sell earlier than the calendar suggests, because every month closer to the age line is a month of runway lost.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Frequently asked questions</h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Which Maruti model has the best resale value in India?</h3>
        <p>The Swift and the Ertiga trade top spots depending on how the question is framed. The Swift holds best among mainstream hatchbacks because it is the segment benchmark and has the deepest buyer pool. The Ertiga holds best in absolute retention across five and seven years, because it carries a second demand layer, tourist-permit and corporate transport buyers, that puts a floor under the private-buyer bid.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does Maruti actually have the best resale value in India?</h3>
        <p>On average, yes, but by a smaller brand-level margin than most buyers assume. The Maruti versus Hyundai average gap is close to 5 percentage points at five years. The gap between the best and worst Maruti at five years is closer to 20. Model choice inside Maruti moves the outcome roughly four times more than the badge choice does.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why do Marutis sell so quickly in the used market?</h3>
        <p>Three reasons: a wide service network that keeps parts and repair costs predictable across the country, a deep second-hand buyer pool in metros and smaller towns alike, and organised buyers who will bid on any clean Maruti. Days-to-sell is short, which is what the used-car market translates into a price premium.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Which Maruti has the worst resale value?</h3>
        <p>Among mainstream models, the Ciaz and the S-Presso currently retain least. The Ciaz reflects a segment-wide contraction in mid-size sedan demand, not a Maruti issue. The S-Presso reflects a young comparable set that leads dealers to bid conservatively. Neither is a durability signal.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does the Swift or the Baleno have better resale value?</h3>
        <p>The Swift retains a higher percentage of its ex-showroom price, but the Baleno starts from a higher price so the rupee loss on the Baleno at five years is often larger. If the question is percentage retention, Swift. If the question is rupees preserved, Swift again, on a lifecycle basis. If the question is monthly ownership cost, the two are closer than the raw percentages suggest.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is factory-fitted CNG better than an aftermarket CNG kit for resale?</h3>
        <p>Yes, and the gap is largely documentary rather than mechanical. Factory CNG is registered on the RC from day one and carries manufacturer warranty history. An aftermarket kit must be endorsed on the RC by the RTO, and when the endorsement is missing the buyer inherits a compliance problem that gets priced as a defect. Company-fitted CNG generally holds a resale premium; retrofitted kits with proper endorsement hold at a modest discount to it; unendorsed kits get discounted hard or refused.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Do Nexa cars have better resale value than Arena cars?</h3>
        <p>Not because of the channel. Nexa models sit in higher price bands where the used buyer pool is smaller and the rupee loss at any given percentage is larger. Percentage retention can look similar across the two channels; the rupees lost are not the same. In tier-3 markets, the sparser Nexa service network can also compress bids on Nexa-badged cars.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does the top variant of a Maruti hold more resale value than the base?</h3>
        <p>In rupee terms, usually less. Used buyers shop by budget band, not by feature list. A top variant that cost ₹2.3 lakh more when new often recovers only ₹80,000 to ₹1,00,000 of that premium at five years. What does recover: automatic gearboxes, factory CNG, and, in compact SUVs specifically, sunroofs. What does not: cosmetic packs, dealer-fitted accessories, extended feature bundles.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">At what age is the best time to sell a Maruti for maximum resale value?</h3>
        <p>Segment matters. Hatchbacks lose the most in Year 1 and flatten around Year 5 to Year 6, so exiting before the flattening captures value that is about to be given up. Compact SUVs decline more steadily, so the "one more year" calculation is closer. On NCR-registered diesels, sell earlier than the calendar suggests, because runway is being lost month by month.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">How much does colour affect a Maruti's resale value?</h3>
        <p>Colour is one of the smaller factors, but it is a real one. White and silver clear fastest and take the smallest condition-adjusted discount. Unusual shades usually cost more in days-to-sell than in headline price, which then shows up in the offer. Paint condition beats paint colour every time.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does a Delhi-registered Maruti sell for less than the same car registered in Bengaluru?</h3>
        <p>Petrol Marutis with substantial remaining registration life mostly trade close, with Delhi generally softer on headline price. Diesel Marutis registered in NCR trade meaningfully lower, because buyers price the shortened runway rather than the mechanical condition.</p>

        <p class="mt-10">Two decisions decide most of what the offer looks like years later: match the variant, fuel and transmission to the city the car will live in, and keep the service record intact from year one. Everything else can be argued about at the inspection bay. Those two cannot be reconstructed on the day of sale.</p>

